From the shared entrance, the flat opens into a central hallway, with the accommodation unfolding naturally towards the rear of the building. The main reception room provides a comfortable and characterful living space, where deep-toned walls, high ceilings and decorative cornicing create a warm, intimate atmosphere. From here, the layout becomes increasingly open, leading through to the dining area and kitchen at the rear.
The kitchen and dining space forms the natural social centre of the home. Dark cabinetry is paired with white metro tiling, open timber shelving and herringbone flooring, while rooflights bring additional light from above. An adjoining dining area, framed by exposed brickwork and banquette seating, creates an informal setting for everyday meals and entertaining. Wide glazed doors open directly onto the garden, allowing the entire rear of the flat to connect naturally with the outside space.
The southeast-facing garden is approximately 34 ft wide. A large paved terrace leads onto an astroturf lawn, with established planting around the boundaries providing ample space for outdoor dining, entertaining and relaxing. The width of the plot gives it the feel of a proper garden rather than simply an outside terrace.
French doors open directly onto the garden from the principal bedroom, creating a wonderfully calm place to unwind. Finished in a striking palette of deep blue, the room incorporates a wall of fitted cabinetry alongside its own open-plan bathing suite, where a freestanding roll-top bath sits within the bedroom itself, complemented by a separate black-framed walk-in shower. Crisp white tiling and traditional detailing provide contrast, resulting in a principal suite that feels both indulgent and highly individual.
Immediately inside the entrance to the flat is a second generous double bedroom, a particularly well-proportioned room with a large arched sash window, decorative cornicing and excellent natural light. Currently arranged to incorporate a workspace, its size makes it well suited to use as a guest bedroom or dedicated home office.
A substantial cellar extends beneath the flat, offering plenty of additional storage. There is a separate shower room with WC positioned off the hallway, ideal for guests, and the flat also has a gated, off-street parking space.
Wandsworth Road places the property between Battersea and Clapham, with an excellent collection of cafés, restaurants, pubs and everyday amenities found throughout the surrounding neighbourhood. Clapham Old Town and Clapham Common are within easy reach, while Battersea Power Station has brought an extensive collection of restaurants and shops to this part of Southwest London.
Larkhall Park provides easy access to green space, with Battersea Park and its riverside walks and recreational facilities also within easy reach. Transport connections are excellent, with Wandsworth Road station providing Overground and National Rail services, Nine Elms offering access to the Northern line, and Stockwell providing both Victoria and Northern line connections across London.

