DESCRIPTION

5a South Park is a substantial duplex apartment located within this Victorian building, occupying the first and second floors. The generously proportioned property, which benefits from high ceilings and lots of natural light, has a private staircase rising externally from the ground floor to a private entrance and comes with a private garden (separate stepped access) and parking for two cars.

The property has been updated and redecorated in the past couple of years, whilst retaining period features including sash windows (recently upgraded to double glazing) and fireplaces.

FEATURES

- The property is approached via a gravel pathway from South Park Private Road to an external brick and tile faced staircase leading up to the first floor private entrance porch, with original Victorian mosaic flooring.

- The spacious and welcoming reception hall immediately makes you forget you are in an apartment with a turned staircase leading up to the bedroom accommodation and panelled pine doors leading into the living rooms and kitchen. Under-stairs storage cupboard.

- The kitchen/breakfast room has been updated with a modern range of wall and base units with quartz worktops. There is a range cooker with gas hob and electric oven, twin Butler-style sink, integrated dishwasher and microwave and space for a tall fridge/freezer.

- Separate utility room with space for a washing machine, tumble drier and under-counter fridge or freezer. Large pantry and sink.

- The beautiful South-West facing drawing room features an original fireplace and has a view out over the garden. Large storage cupboard.

- The study is accessed from the drawing room and is open plan to the dining room, which in turn leads back around to the reception hall. The dining room has a projecting bay window overlooking the garden and another feature fireplace.

- Cloakroom.

- From the second floor landing, a glass door opens out onto a private South-East facing balcony, with a lofty view over South Park.

- The dual aspect principal bedroom has a projecting bay with windows overlooking the garden and towards the Kippington estate. Extensive range of fitted wardrobes and drawers. En-suite bathroom.

- There are two further generous double bedrooms, both with feature fire places, and storage/fitted wardrobes/ shelves.

- Separate shower room. Both the bath and shower rooms have been updated with modern white suites.

OUTSIDE

- Steps lead down to the side of the building to a private South-West facing garden with an expanse of lawn, herbaceous borders and a decked seating/dining area. Garden shed.

- There is a large storage cupboard under the access staircase, ideal for storing garden implements/sporting equipment etc.

- Two off-road parking spaces for the sole use of and adjacent to the property.

SITUATION

The property is conveniently positioned within a short walk of Sevenoaks town centre, with its wide selection of shops, supermarkets (including Waitrose and M&S), restaurants, pubs and cafes.

Sevenoaks mainline rail station is just over half a mile away with fast and frequent services to London Bridge, Charing Cross and Cannon Street (London Bridge from about 24 minutes).

Leisure facilities in the area include the Stag cinema/theatre, Sevenoaks Leisure Centre, cricket at The Vine, tennis, rugby and football clubs, golf courses at Knole and Wildernesse and Nizels Golf and Fitness Centre in Hildenborough.

Knole Park is just over half a mile from the property offering acres of National Trust deer parkland with public footpaths.

There are excellent state and private schools in and around the town including nearby St Thomas Catholic Primary, Sevenoaks Primary, Sevenoaks Prep, New Beacon, Granville, Solefield, Trinity School, Knole Academy, Walthamstow Hall, Weald of Kent Grammar School and the renowned International Sevenoaks School. There are also grammar and state schools at Tonbridge and Tunbridge Wells.

The M25 (Junction 5) is 3.1 miles north of Sevenoaks providing links to the National Motorway Network, Gatwick, Heathrow and Stansted airports, The Channel Tunnel, Ebbsfleet International and Bluewater shopping centre.

DIRECTIONS

From Sevenoaks station turn right onto London Road heading south, and then immediately right onto Granville Road. Continue up this road for about half a mile, passing Argyle Road on the left-hand side. Immediately after, turn right into a private road (before St Thomas School) where 5a South Park will be found on the right-hand side.

PROPERTY INFORMATION

- Tenure: Leasehold.
- Lease: 189 years from 24 June 1979 (143 years remaining
- Ground rent: nil
- Service charge: to be agreed and shared proportionately between the four flats. 5A currently pays a 50% contribution and this has amounted to about £1,700 per year in the last 3 years.
- Additional charges: £84 per annum private road charge
- Services: Mains gas fired central heating. Mains water, electricity and drainage.
- Local Authority: Sevenoaks District Council
- Council Tax band: E (£2,867.25 for 2024/25)
- Fixtures and fittings are excluded from the sale but may be available by separate negotiation.

The property is conveniently positioned within a short walk of Sevenoaks town centre, with its wide selection of shops, supermarkets (including Waitrose and M&S), restaurants, pubs and cafes.

Sevenoaks mainline rail station is just over half a mile away with fast and frequent services to London Bridge, Charing Cross and Cannon Street (London Bridge from about 24 minutes).

Leisure facilities in the area include the Stag cinema/theatre, Sevenoaks Leisure Centre, cricket at The Vine, tennis, rugby and football clubs, golf courses at Knole and Wildernesse and Nizels Golf and Fitness Centre in Hildenborough.

Knole Park is just over half a mile from the property offering acres of National Trust deer parkland with public footpaths.

There are excellent state and private schools in and around the town including nearby St Thomas Catholic Primary, Sevenoaks Primary, Sevenoaks Prep, New Beacon, Granville, Solefield, Trinity School, Knole Academy, Walthamstow Hall, Weald of Kent Grammar School and the renowned International Sevenoaks School. There are also grammar and state schools at Tonbridge and Tunbridge Wells.

The M25 (Junction 5) is 3.1 miles north of Sevenoaks providing links to the National Motorway Network, Gatwick, Heathrow and Stansted airports, The Channel Tunnel, Ebbsfleet International and Bluewater shopping centre.', 'Jackson-Stops, Kent And East Sussex', '2026-10-09T00:26:45Z', '2026-10-24T10:17:01Z', '[]', 'train');
A striking, individually designed five-bedroom detached home occupying an elevated position on one of Saltdean’s most desirable residential roads. Bold architecture, beautifully individual interiors and an ingenious reverse-level arrangement combine to create a home that feels unmistakably different.
From the street, the house immediately sets its stall out. Crisp rendered elevations, contemporary cladding, dark-framed glazing and an asymmetric roofline give it a distinctive architectural presence, while the broad frontage provides off-road parking alongside the integral garage. Above, glass-fronted balconies hint at what really defines this house: light, outlook and the sea. Solar panels are discreetly integrated into the roof, adding an energy-conscious element to the property while complementing its contemporary architectural character.
The accommodation has been deliberately arranged to make the most of its elevated setting, with the principal living space occupying the first floor. It is here that the house becomes something genuinely special.
A spectacular vaulted kitchen, dining and living room forms the social heart of the home. The soaring ceilings and extensive glazing create an extraordinary sense of volume, while large doors draw the eye beyond the room towards the balcony, surrounding green space and stunning outlook.
The kitchen has been designed with character rather than convention. Rich green cabinetry, pale worktops and warm metallic accents sit alongside open timber shelving and abundant planting, while the substantial island is wrapped in reclaimed timber laid in a distinctive chevron pattern. Sculptural suspended lighting and a dramatic stainless-steel extractor create a sophisticated architectural focal point above.
There is space here to cook, dine, entertain and relax without any part of the room feeling compromised. The sitting area naturally gravitates towards the glazing, where doors open onto the broad balcony and transform the room during warmer months. It is a wonderfully sociable space, but equally compelling when the house is quiet and the changing coastal outlook becomes the focus.
The first floor also provides two generous bedrooms. The principal bedroom enjoys its own en-suite shower room, while an additional WC serves the second bedroom and living accommodation. This arrangement gives the upper floor an appealing degree of independence and places the principal sleeping accommodation alongside the best views in the house.
The ground floor reveals just how versatile the property is. Three further double bedrooms provide excellent accommodation for children, guests or multigenerational living, with one benefitting from its own en suite shower room. One of the bedrooms could equally be reimagined as a substantial study, gym or second reception room, offering exceptional flexibility to suit a range of lifestyles.
The family bathroom is full of personality, combining deep green walls with traditional-style sanitaryware, a freestanding roll-top bath and separate glazed shower. Elsewhere, the interiors continue to avoid the predictable, with another beautifully finished shower room introducing soft blush tones, patterned stone-effect flooring and warm brass detailing.
Practicality has not been forgotten, but even the utility spaces have been conceived with entertaining and everyday living in mind. A discreetly fitted hall cupboard has been designed to house the washing machine and tumble dryer, keeping laundry appliances neatly tucked away and freeing the additional ground-floor space for something rather more sociable.
Known within the house as the ‘Snug’, this versatile room was designed as a second lounge, combining comfortable living space with useful cabinetry and integrated appliances. Bi-fold doors open directly onto the garden, creating a seamless connection between the room and outside space and making it particularly well suited to summer entertaining, family gatherings or simply relaxing with the garden beyond.
The integral garage offers useful parking and storage, while the solar panels provide an additional practical benefit, contributing to the home''s energy efficiency and reinforcing its thoughtful contemporary credentials.
Outside, the detached garden office adds another valuable dimension. Completely separate from the main house, it lends itself perfectly to home working, a creative studio or a quiet retreat without sacrificing a bedroom.
But ultimately, this house is about its relationship with its surroundings. The elevated balcony becomes an extension of the living room, overlooking open green space and carrying the view towards the coast. With relatively little between the house and the horizon, the outlook has an unusually open quality. The sea is not simply glimpsed from an upstairs window; it forms part of the everyday backdrop to the home.
Individual, substantial and refreshingly unconventional, Arundel Drive East offers the space of a serious family house with the atmosphere of a contemporary coastal retreat, complemented by solar panels and thoughtful modern design, and is offered with no onward chain

